# Intro
A sheet of stars inside uniform low-metallicity gas, with the ISRF transport on
(`ISRF_propagation: 1`) and the H2 line absorption of the transport on
(`ISRF_H2_line_absorption: 1`). Sixteen 4.9 Msun stars sit on a square grid,
0.844 pc apart, in the mid-plane of a periodic box of 3.38 x 3.38 x 6.76 pc.
The gas has n_H = 1000 cm^-3, T = 100 K and Z = 0.25 Zsun, and starts atomic.

The Lyman-Werner (LW) light of the stars dissociates H2, and H2 forms on dust.
An H2 layer grows on each side of the sheet. A reference slab model predicts
its front (half the hydrogen in H2) about 1.46 pc from the sheet after 12 Myr;
the example does not check the run against this number. Light reaches a gas particle
from many stars at wide angles, so the H2 column that shields it differs from
ray to ray. The surface luminosity puts the atomic layer at a dust optical depth
of about 1.7, which slab theory (Sternberg et al. 2014, ApJ 790, 10, Eq. 40)
writes as ln(alpha G / 2 + 1) with alpha G of about 8.7.

The gas stays at rest: `GeajayCooling:with_radiative_cooling: 0` lets the
chemistry evolve but keeps the internal energy, so the pressure stays uniform.
The temperature then rises only through the mean molecular weight, by up to 86
per cent in fully molecular gas. Photoionization, winds, supernovae and the
UV background are off. The stars live about 90 Myr, longer than the run, and
keep their young time step (`Stars:timestep_age_threshold_Myr`).

# Configure
```
./configure --with-chemistry=GEAR_10 --with-feedback=GEAR --with-cooling=geajay_3 --with-stars=GEAR --with-sink=GEAR --with-star-formation=GEAR --with-tracers=GEAR --with-kernel=wendland-C2 --with-geajay=path/to/geajay --with-chealpix=path/to/chealpix
make -j
```
Use geajay commit `41b559b8` or later (library version `libgeajay.so.3`): the
build must read the keys `GeajayCooling:with_radiative_cooling` and
`GeajayCooling:solver`. `run.sh` fetches the radiation table
`PopII_parsec_spectral.hdf5` and checks its checksum; the table must carry
`L_LW_dissociating`, which `ISRF_LW_population_dissociation: 1` reads.

# Run
```
./run.sh                 # the cooling reads the kernel mean of the light (default)
leg=own ./run.sh         # the cooling reads the particle's own light
```
Each leg is kept in its own directory (`main`, `own`). `level`, `gas_mass`,
`gas_density`, `star_mass`, `n_threads` and `swift_exe` override the defaults.
The box side follows from `level` and `gas_mass`, and the number of stars per
side from the box side and the spacing of 0.844 pc, so the surface luminosity
stays the same:

| `level` | `gas_mass` [Msun] | gas particles | h [pc] | box side [pc] | stars |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| 5 | 0.03825 | 65536 | 0.13 | 3.38 | 16 |
| 4 | 0.306 | 8192 | 0.26 | 3.38 | 16 |
| 4 | 2.448 | 8192 | 0.52 | 6.76 | 64 |
| 4 | 19.58 | 8192 | 1.04 | 13.51 | 256 |
| 4 | 156.6 | 8192 | 2.08 | 27.02 | 1024 |

At h = 0.13 pc the front lies about 5.8 kernel supports (1.936 h) from the
sheet; at h = 1.04 pc and above it lies inside one kernel. A box side below
about six smoothing lengths stops SWIFT at start-up (the neighbour search must
fit in a third of the box), so the coarse rows widen the box. The run lasts
12 Myr and writes a snapshot every 0.25 Myr.

# Output
`makeIC.py` writes `fixture.json`: the box, the height of the sheet
(`sheet_z_kpc`), the star positions and spacing, and the gas density. In the
snapshots, `PartType0/H2I` over the hydrogen mass fraction gives the H2
fraction, and the distance |z - sheet_z_kpc| the depth of a particle.
`LWOwnSpecificEnergies` is the particle's own LW energy without the lines;
`LWOwnDissociatingSpecificEnergies` over it, divided by the star's dissociation
factor (`L_LW_dissociating / L_LW` of the radiation table, 0.978 for these
stars), is the line transmission. `LWLineH2ColumnDensities` is the H2 column that the light
carried. `LWSpecificEnergies` is the LW energy that the cooling reads: the
kernel mean in the leg `main`, the particle's own light in the leg `own`.
